Looking over my previous post, it seems rather incoherent.  Let me try
to put it more succinctly.

By the time the player reaches the Castle, the player often has most
of the things he needs to win the game.  If he doesn't before the
castle, the wand of wishing all but ensures he will after.

Why does the player scour each and every level before the Castle? 
Because he's looking for stuff, either lying around, in a shop, or in
a monster's inventory, that will help him succeed in the game.  A good
piece of equipment is more important, in terms of game usefulness,
than an experience level, and harder to acquire.

Nethack remains interesting while the player is still looking for
stuff he needs and has a chance of gaining it, and while actually
making use of them.  And many of the most desired kit-provided
advantages can be gained in multiple ways.

Thus, what I'm suggesting is: Leave the player to rely more on the
random number generator and the law of averages to get most of the
stuff he needs, instead of "guaranteed" items.  I understand that the
Gnomish Mines, Sokoban and the Quest need a reward at the end, but I
have qualms with including a specific, guaranteed item that isn't
directly needed to win the game.  That's why the suggestion about
toning down the number of wishes from the wand, it's too good an item
to grant when there's still around 25 levels to explore going down,
then around 50 to pass through going up, especially since most players
already have most of what they need to win by this point, especially
if they've been through the Mines, Sokoban, the Quest, Medusa, etc.

But, to try to poke holes in my own suggestion, toning down the item
generation has a side effect in that it means an unlucky player will
have problems if he's explored every level and not found what he
needs.  This is the hardest thing about the original Rogue, or at
least the PC version I've had the most experience with, that unless
the player is really lucky he's not going to find the specific stuff
that will enable him to survive.

So my full suggestion is:
* To reduce the wishes from the wand.  At the moment, I think the wand
is generated with between one and three charges, and can be recharged
once.  Someone examine this statement for accuracy: that's between
four and six wishes for a player who knows what he's doing, plus one
if he's already got or can get a blessed scroll of charging.  Wish
sources: 1-3 from original charges, 3 from recharge, one from
wresting.  That's D/SDSM, boots of speed, magic resistance and an
amulet of life saving or gauntlets of power right there, and many
players already have at least two of those things.

* Add at least one long, optional dungeon branch earlier in the game,
maybe at around Dlvl 16, with only a minor reward at the end.  The
purpose of this branch is to give the player extra opportunities to
gain necessary equipment without requiring a visit if the player
doesn't need it.  This will also give the player more opportunity to
find shops (which can be downright rare sometimes) and/or altars, and
can also help him out if he's hurting for experience before the Quest.
 Maybe some of Slash'em's vast number of special levels could be
included in there to liven it up.

* Shorten the mazes, *and* make them more interesting.  I understand
that the purpose of the mazes is to give the player something to sweat
through after he gets the Amulet and has to actually work his way up
to the entrance.  I like the idea of maze-levels (and think it'd be
cool to see them occasionally in the early floors!), but they get
annoying when almost half the game is composed of them, without shops,
altars, or most of the other things that make the Dungeons of Doom
such a fun hangout spot.  I understand this is a big part of the
motivation behind the Lethe patch....

There, maybe that'll be a little less obtuse.

